VI  - 7
IP  - 15
DP  - 2019 Aug
TI  - Postobstructive pneumonia in lung cancer.
PG  - 357
LID - 10.21037/atm.2019.05.26 [doi]
LID - 357
AB  - Postobstructive pneumonia can complicate lung cancer, particularly in more 
      advanced stages of the disease, producing significant clinical decline and a 
      poorer prognosis. It can lead to complications such as empyema, lung abscess and 
      fistula formation. Postobstructive pneumonia can also be the first manifestation 
      of an underlying malignancy. There are multiple challenges in the management of 
      these patients. Recognition and treatment of this entity can be complex and 
      includes the use of imaging, administration of broad-spectrum antibiotics to 
      cover the wide variety of microorganisms involved and the use of different 
      interventional modalities to relieve the obstruction. Existing literature on 
      postobstructive pneumonia is scarce. In this article, we review the 
      pathophysiology, different diagnostic methods and the therapeutic options to 
      treat this condition. The utility and efficacy of the various modalities that are 
      currently available in clinical practice to the interventional pulmonologist are 
      described in some detail.
