IS  - 2642-0007 (Linking)
VI  - 7
IP  - 7
DP  - 2026 Jul
TI  - The Procedure-Only Transfer Pathway - An Alternative to Standard Interhospital 
      Transfer to Address Time-Sensitive Procedural Needs.
PG  - CAT250348
LID - 10.1056/CAT.25.0348 [doi]
AB  - Minimally invasive procedures - performed by health care professionals in 
      gastroenterology, interventional radiology, and electrophysiology - are now 
      standard of care for management of many inpatient conditions. However, the 
      physical infrastructure and technical expertise required to perform these 
      interventions are increasingly concentrated within urban and academic centers. 
      Patients with advanced endoscopic or electrophysiologic needs who are 
      hospitalized at rural or community hospitals are thus dependent upon expedient 
      interhospital transfer for access to needed procedures. As hospital capacity has 
      become tighter, those patients awaiting transfer experience prolonged wait times, 
      leading to care delays and even suboptimal outcomes. In response, Oregon Health 
      and Science University created the procedure-only pathway, through which patients 
      are transferred from the referring hospital to the tertiary care center solely 
      for procedural intervention, after which they are immediately transported back to 
      the referring institution. This alternative to traditional interhospital transfer 
      was designed to be patient-centered, facilitate access to scarce resources, and 
      preserve beds at the academic center for those with ongoing tertiary care needs. 
      Extensive multidisciplinary collaboration was required to create safe, facile 
      workflows for this process. Recurrent feedback from stakeholders, including 
      hospitalists, proceduralists, anesthesia, billing, and the transfer center team 
      was imperative to refine the pathway. While the administrative hurdles were high, 
      they were not insurmountable, and the program yielded significant benefits for 
      patients and hospitals alike. The innovation led to markedly decreased wait times 
      for inpatient procedures. Over a 3-year period, patients arriving via the 
      procedure-only pathway obtained indicated procedures 1.52 days after the initial 
      transfer call, compared with 4.99 days for those transferred by the standard 
      route. No significant increase in complications was observed among the 
      procedure-only cohort. The annual volume of procedure-only cases continues to 
      grow as the billing, legal, and transportation infrastructure has matured. While 
      only eight such cases were performed in 2021, 93 were completed in 2025. Finally, 
      as procedure-only transfers ramped up, the authors observed a proportional 
      decrease in the number of patients waitlisted to their institution but ultimately 
      transferred to another hospital system owing to prolonged delays for beds. The 
      procedure-only pathway keeps high-value procedures within the system. Because of 
      the success of the procedure-only pilot, Oregon Health and Science University is 
      creating contracts with additional referring hospitals and expanding its 
      inclusion criteria to additional procedural types - including interventional 
      pulmonology and diagnostic radiology.
